WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.600
Film Courage: Let’s suppose you are encountering
arrogance from someone and really what you

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:07.060
said before was that it was fear (also too,
their ego).

00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:11.019
Their pride could be hurt because they are
thinking “Why do you think I don’t know

00:00:11.019 --> 00:00:12.019
what I am doing?”

00:00:12.019 --> 00:00:15.510
Which is not really what you are saying, you’re
just wanting to explore more possibilities

00:00:15.510 --> 00:00:18.420
and let them know that you have a class and
coaching available.

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:24.039
But how can you turn it around and have it
so that they don’t feel hurt (that it’s

00:00:24.039 --> 00:00:25.090
about their abilities)?

00:00:25.090 --> 00:00:27.470
Mark W. Travis: Okay, that’s a great question.

00:00:27.470 --> 00:00:31.360
First of all we go back to resistance and
the obstacle thing we talked about before

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:35.739
because if I attack them or approach them,
that whole thing about their arrogance and

00:00:35.739 --> 00:00:42.020
they’re putting up a wall against other
possibilities, the wall will get stronger.

00:00:42.020 --> 00:00:43.579
So I can’t do that.

00:00:43.579 --> 00:00:45.870
But if I say “I know how to work with actors.

00:00:45.870 --> 00:00:47.710
I know all of that.”

00:00:47.710 --> 00:00:49.710
Usually I will say “Great…great.

00:00:49.710 --> 00:00:52.840
Let’s see how this goes.”

00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:57.989
And then, usually what happens and I’ve
seen this and Elsha’s seen this many times,

00:00:57.989 --> 00:01:02.930
start working with a director and start working
with the interrogation process, two things

00:01:02.930 --> 00:01:04.170
happen very quickly.

00:01:04.170 --> 00:01:09.209
First of all, they realize that the interrogation
process is difficult and that’s a little

00:01:09.209 --> 00:01:10.209
intimidating.

00:01:10.209 --> 00:01:11.209
But they hang in.

00:01:11.209 --> 00:01:15.979
But the other thing that they see is what
starts to come out of the characters and they

00:01:15.979 --> 00:01:23.600
start to see that they are now experiencing
something way beyond what they imagined and

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:25.929
it probably includes what they thought they
wanted.

00:01:25.929 --> 00:01:28.259
It includes aspects of the character that
they want.

00:01:28.259 --> 00:01:29.460
But they start to see more.

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:36.200
They see such a rich terrain in front of them
that suddenly the arrogance drops away.

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:40.899
So I rely on the power of what I am teaching,
the techniques of what I am teaching if I

00:01:40.899 --> 00:01:49.789
can take them through these skills and allow
them to experience it, it will change.

00:01:49.789 --> 00:01:55.139
There’s another similar situation, when
we get into staging which we talked about

00:01:55.139 --> 00:01:59.739
before and when I’m doing a workshop on
staging and then a director is like “Oh…I

00:01:59.739 --> 00:02:00.960
know how to stage this thing.”

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:02.219
I said “Great.”

00:02:02.219 --> 00:02:06.340
And they’ll stage it and I’ll look at
it and I’ll think “No, there’s much

00:02:06.340 --> 00:02:07.819
more you can explore.”

00:02:07.819 --> 00:02:12.079
But rather than tell them “No, you haven’t
explored enough.

00:02:12.079 --> 00:02:14.069
You don’t get it.”

00:02:14.069 --> 00:02:16.379
I very gently say “Okay, that’s great.”

00:02:16.379 --> 00:02:20.580
And I’ll say to the actor how do you feel
standing there (in the scene).

00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:21.790
And they’ll go “I feel fine, fine…”

00:02:21.790 --> 00:02:22.790
Film Courage: Uh oh…fine?

00:02:22.790 --> 00:02:28.150
Mark W. Travis: And I’ll say to the director
“Okay, she’s standing there at the beginning

00:02:28.150 --> 00:02:30.609
of the scene and how do you want her to feel?”

00:02:30.609 --> 00:02:32.590
“She should feel really insecure.”

00:02:32.590 --> 00:02:36.170
And I say “Okay” and I move the actor
a little bit to another place.

00:02:36.170 --> 00:02:37.250
And I said “Now, how do you feel?”

00:02:37.250 --> 00:02:39.170
“This is awful, I felt terrible over there.”

00:02:39.170 --> 00:02:41.200
And I turn to the director and I say “Is
that what you want?”

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:42.200
He says “Yeah.”

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:44.060
And I say “You’ve got it.”

00:02:44.060 --> 00:02:45.170
Then he sees “Wow.

00:02:45.170 --> 00:02:47.470
You did that just by moving her?”

00:02:47.470 --> 00:02:56.099
Rather than argue about what he knows and
what will work, my job is to demonstrate to

00:02:56.099 --> 00:02:58.980
the directors “Here’s how this works.

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:04.239
You can argue whether you need it or not,
you can tell me…but let me show you how

00:03:04.239 --> 00:03:05.299
it works.”

00:03:05.299 --> 00:03:08.739
90% of the time directors are blown away.

00:03:08.739 --> 00:03:14.590
We had a situation here yesterday with a writer.

00:03:14.590 --> 00:03:18.049
This is a slightly different thing but on
a script.

00:03:18.049 --> 00:03:24.790
And I interrogated the writer as the main
character in the script and it took (I don’t

00:03:24.790 --> 00:03:28.909
know Elsha, what was it about 3 to 5 minutes?)

00:03:28.909 --> 00:03:35.860
5 minutes…5 minutes later, first of all
he was in tears as the character.

00:03:35.860 --> 00:03:44.109
He was shaking and in tears because of what
the character was experiencing and then the

00:03:44.109 --> 00:03:49.010
writer, what he was experiencing through the
character and went “Oh, my Gosh.”

00:03:49.010 --> 00:03:55.420
And he realized he had a whole different movie,
he had a whole different scenario.

00:03:55.420 --> 00:03:59.849
Now he starts to understand the power of what
I’m trying to teach him.

00:03:59.849 --> 00:04:03.569
I could try to explain that to him and go
I don’t need that.

00:04:03.569 --> 00:04:10.920
So writers, directors and actors have to experience
these techniques.

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:15.439
I can write about it forever, you can see
this interview, you can watch this and go

00:04:15.439 --> 00:04:16.439
“I think I’ve got it.

00:04:16.439 --> 00:04:17.750
I think I know what he is talking about.”

00:04:17.750 --> 00:04:21.870
You could even watch the DVD that you watched
and once you are in there, once you are in

00:04:21.870 --> 00:04:26.900
the room experiencing it as a writer, as a
director, as an actor, any of these techniques,

00:04:26.900 --> 00:04:27.900
then you will know it.

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:29.419
It has to be experienced.

00:04:29.419 --> 00:04:31.150
Film Courage: Mark…fine.

00:04:31.150 --> 00:04:37.100
I’m hearing that this is a word that doesn’t
have a good connotation to it.

00:04:37.100 --> 00:04:38.100
What does F.I.N.E.

00:04:38.100 --> 00:04:39.100
mean to you?

00:04:39.100 --> 00:04:41.460
Mark W. Travis: When did I use fine?

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:43.020
Film Courage: A couple of times [during this
segment].

00:04:43.020 --> 00:04:47.050
And maybe I’m projecting my own worldview
onto it.

00:04:47.050 --> 00:04:52.690
But (possible, probably) I am hearing that
“fine” is not a good word to describe

00:04:52.690 --> 00:04:53.690
something.

00:04:53.690 --> 00:04:57.000
In fact, it means the opposite but someone’s
afraid to let someone know that “I’m not

00:04:57.000 --> 00:04:58.000
comfortable with it.”

00:04:58.000 --> 00:04:59.000
“How are you today?”

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:00.000
“Uh…oh…I’m fine.”

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:02.789
Mark W. Travis: Oh, I see when someone says
“I’m fine.”

00:05:02.789 --> 00:05:04.759
Film Courage: “How was that one scene?”

00:05:04.759 --> 00:05:05.759
“Uh…yeah, fine.

00:05:05.759 --> 00:05:06.759
It’s fine.”

00:05:06.759 --> 00:05:07.759
“How was the script?”

00:05:07.759 --> 00:05:08.759
“Oh yeah we did some note revisions.

00:05:08.759 --> 00:05:09.759
It’s fine.”

00:05:09.759 --> 00:05:11.419
Mark W. Travis: Let me ask you…this is not
an interview on you but…

00:05:11.419 --> 00:05:13.509
Film Courage: Uh oh.

00:05:13.509 --> 00:05:19.180
Mark W. Travis: When you hear someone…Karen…when
you say to someone “How are you doing?”

00:05:19.180 --> 00:05:20.180
“Oh, I’m fine.”

00:05:20.180 --> 00:05:21.180
What do you hear?

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:26.500
Film Courage: I hear that things are not good
but they don’t want to say it, so they have

00:05:26.500 --> 00:05:32.410
to have a good…a cover that’s sort of
medium instead of “Oh, I’m doing great

00:05:32.410 --> 00:05:33.640
today!”

00:05:33.640 --> 00:05:37.410
Or “Oh, I had a horrible time getting here
but I’m fine.”

00:05:37.410 --> 00:05:38.950
They want to close it down.

00:05:38.950 --> 00:05:39.950
They don’t want to talk anymore.

00:05:39.950 --> 00:05:40.950
Mark W. Travis: Yep.

00:05:40.950 --> 00:05:44.590
And they probably saying (what you are probably
hearing) is “I’m fine.

00:05:44.590 --> 00:05:47.400
I’m doing okay and I don’t want to talk
anymore.”

00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:54.160
Which makes you think and me think there’s
a lot going on there that is uncomfortable,

00:05:54.160 --> 00:05:59.800
she or he doesn’t want to go there so when
you say to an actor or someone you work with

00:05:59.800 --> 00:06:00.810
“How are you doing today?”

00:06:00.810 --> 00:06:01.810
“Fine.”

00:06:01.810 --> 00:06:03.220
And you go, okay there is something going
on.

00:06:03.220 --> 00:06:08.700
Now if you keep pressing up against that wall,
you’re going to get a lot of resistance.

00:06:08.700 --> 00:06:12.080
Now your job is to “Let me see, can I get
to it?

00:06:12.080 --> 00:06:14.750
Can I find another way?”

00:06:14.750 --> 00:06:18.229
And this has been pretty much my whole career.

00:06:18.229 --> 00:06:22.490
Find another way to access the truth?”

00:06:22.490 --> 00:06:27.909
With that individual or with that character,
with that writer, director, or actor.

00:06:27.909 --> 00:06:33.139
How can I get around the barriers that are
automatically there because we’re human

00:06:33.139 --> 00:06:34.930
beings and we put them up?

00:06:34.930 --> 00:06:36.910
We protect ourselves.

00:06:36.910 --> 00:06:42.310
Can I find another way appropriately to get
around that and get to the truth, not only

00:06:42.310 --> 00:06:50.830
so I can see the truth but so that the person
who said “Fine,”…let’s say it’s

00:06:50.830 --> 00:06:52.530
an actor who said fine.

00:06:52.530 --> 00:06:57.539
My feeling is that there is also a part of
that person who wants to reveal that, you

00:06:57.539 --> 00:06:58.539
know?

00:06:58.539 --> 00:07:02.860
That’s there committee, there’s another
voice who says “Don’t talk about it.”

00:07:02.860 --> 00:07:07.490
In other words there’s a little war going
on in there that we all experience.

00:07:07.490 --> 00:07:13.990
So if I can get to the truth and some how
find a way, not only so I can know more, but

00:07:13.990 --> 00:07:19.620
so also he or she can have the opportunity
to reveal it or even learn something about

00:07:19.620 --> 00:07:21.260
themselves, quite possibly.

00:07:21.260 --> 00:07:23.550
Film Courage: Okay.

00:07:23.550 --> 00:07:27.689
So fine to you doesn’t mean conversations
over and it’s good.

00:07:27.689 --> 00:07:31.300
It means “Hmmm, I think something’s wrong.

00:07:31.300 --> 00:07:35.360
But I think they might be willing to talk
about it if I go around a different way.

00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:39.820
And I don’t keep pushing up against “It
doesn’t sound like things are fine?”

00:07:39.820 --> 00:07:43.599
Mark W. Travis: Yeah…if I do “It doesn’t
sound like things are fine” then the wall

00:07:43.599 --> 00:07:45.699
is going to get thicker.

00:07:45.699 --> 00:07:48.189
And it might even be someone says something
and I say “How are you doing?”

00:07:48.189 --> 00:07:51.729
And they are “fine” and okay, we are going
to get back to that later.

00:07:51.729 --> 00:07:59.120
And just go off on a whole other track, but
with the intention of getting to what’s

00:07:59.120 --> 00:08:01.360
bothering them.

00:08:01.360 --> 00:08:06.270
If it’s an actor, since we’re talking
a lot about working with actors, if it’s

00:08:06.270 --> 00:08:11.659
an actor, then my concern will be that whatever’s
really going on is going to get in the way

00:08:11.659 --> 00:08:13.389
of their work that day.

00:08:13.389 --> 00:08:17.770
If it’s an actor I’m working with that
day “How are you doing?”

00:08:17.770 --> 00:08:18.770
“Fine.”

00:08:18.770 --> 00:08:22.710
Okay…this could get in the way of the work
we are doing today.

00:08:22.710 --> 00:08:30.289
And let’s say I am picking up on resentment
or anger or fear and these are very general.

00:08:30.289 --> 00:08:36.581
And let’s say it’s Ian (an actor I’ve
worked with a lot) and I get this “Fine.”

00:08:36.581 --> 00:08:38.270
And I go “Okay, there’s a lot of this
going on.”

00:08:38.270 --> 00:08:43.140
Now, as we’re working on the scene, I haven’t
addressed it at all, we’re working on a

00:08:43.140 --> 00:08:46.560
scene and I start interrogating his character.

00:08:46.560 --> 00:08:49.170
Do you know where I’ll go?

00:08:49.170 --> 00:08:52.530
Anger, resentment, fear…as the character!

00:08:52.530 --> 00:08:59.090
I will allow Ian to express whatever’s going
on through the character and this is when

00:08:59.090 --> 00:09:02.910
it actually becomes in a way a little therapeutic
which is another whole topic because there

00:09:02.910 --> 00:09:07.730
are therapists who have been looking at what
I’m doing and wonder if it’s another form

00:09:07.730 --> 00:09:09.470
of therapy.

00:09:09.470 --> 00:09:12.170
Which I will address right now…It’s not.

00:09:12.170 --> 00:09:19.750
It’s not a form of therapy because I’ve
had a lot people with the hypnosis question

00:09:19.750 --> 00:09:22.500
but the interrogation is not a form of therapy.

00:09:22.500 --> 00:09:27.880
A therapy (generally) is intended to help
the patient, the person, the individual, sort

00:09:27.880 --> 00:09:32.920
of smooth out the edges, get through life
in a much more comfortable way and deal with

00:09:32.920 --> 00:09:35.610
whatever problems he’s dealing with.

00:09:35.610 --> 00:09:38.790
Interrogation is absolutely the opposite.

00:09:38.790 --> 00:09:41.210
Interrogation creates chaos.

00:09:41.210 --> 00:09:47.450
I create chaos inside the character so that
the actor (as the character) has to struggle

00:09:47.450 --> 00:09:48.920
with…I want them to struggle!

00:09:48.920 --> 00:09:54.910
I don’t want to smooth it out and I want
the actor to experience the struggle that’s

00:09:54.910 --> 00:10:00.170
going on inside the characters, so it’s
the opposite of therapy.

00:10:00.170 --> 00:10:04.140
Coming back to your question about the fine(s),
someone saying fine.

00:10:04.140 --> 00:10:10.640
I think we all hear that there is a hidden
feeling, emotions, or truth behind that.

00:10:10.640 --> 00:10:17.060
And our job as directors is to honor that,
respect it but not forget it.

00:10:17.060 --> 00:10:25.640
And if we can allow the actors (especially)
a way to release that through the characters,

00:10:25.640 --> 00:10:30.070
even if it’s inappropriate for the character
at the time, that may be a gift to the actor.

